  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "id": "how-a-picture-directs-the-eye",
  "title": "How a Picture Directs the Eye",
  "category": "Art",
  "large": true,
  "info": {
    "text": "Composition shapes what viewers notice, how they understand spatial relationships, and where their attention travels through an image.",
    "link": "wiki:Composition (visual arts)"
  },
  "relatedPuzzles": {
    "info": {
      "text": "These puzzles develop complementary ways of looking closely at visual art."
    },
    "entries": [
      {
        "puzzle": {
          "@id": "urn:concept-clusters:puzzle:reading-a-painting"
        },
        "reason": "Apply this closer study of composition within a broader interpretation of a painting's visual elements and historical setting."
      }
    ]
  },
  "learningIntroduction": {
    "requirement": "recommended",
    "title": "Learning to Follow the Eye",
    "summary": "Notice how relationships among size, position, contrast, empty space, and repeated forms can guide attention through an image.",
    "estimatedMinutes": 4,
    "revision": 1,
    "content": {
      "text": "# Learning to Follow the Eye\n\nLooking at a picture may feel immediate, but seeing is not a single, neutral act. Your attention moves. Some parts stand out quickly, some are noticed later, and some may remain almost invisible until you deliberately search for them.\n\nArtists and designers can influence this experience through **composition**: the arrangement of visual elements within an image.\n\n## Attention is relational\n\nAn object does not attract attention only because of what it is. It may stand out because it differs from its surroundings.\n\nA light form can become prominent against a dark field. A small shape may draw attention when everything around it is large. A lone figure may stand out because it is separated from a crowd.\n\nThis means that visual prominence often belongs not to one element by itself, but to a **relationship** among elements.\n\n## A flat surface can suggest space\n\nA painting, drawing, photograph, or screen is physically flat, yet it can suggest nearness, distance, enclosure, openness, and depth.\n\nViewers use clues such as:\n\n- one form partly covering another;\n- differences in apparent size;\n- the division between nearer and more distant areas;\n- open areas surrounding objects; and\n- lines that seem to continue into the image.\n\nThese clues do not create actual depth. They organize marks on a surface so that depth can be perceived or imagined.\n\n## Balance is not the same as symmetry\n\nA symmetrical image may feel stable because similar forms appear on opposite sides. But an image can also feel balanced without matching halves.\n\nA large, quiet form might be balanced by several small, active ones. A dark area might counter a larger but lighter area. An object near the edge may create more tension than the same object near the center.\n\nWhat matters is not only physical size. Viewers also experience **visual weight**: the strength with which an area attracts and holds attention.\n\n## Still images can imply movement\n\nNothing in a still image literally moves, but the arrangement can make the eye travel.\n\nLines may point toward a subject. Repeated shapes may establish a sequence. Diagonal forms may seem more active than horizontal ones. A body caught in an unstable pose may imply what happened just before the depicted moment and what may happen next.\n\nThe image remains still while the viewer experiences direction, continuation, and momentum.\n\n## Guidance is not control\n\nComposition influences attention, but it does not determine a single identical response in every viewer.\n\nPeople bring different experiences, purposes, cultural knowledge, visual abilities, and habits of attention. The same arrangement can therefore support more than one path through an image.\n\nCareful looking asks both:\n\n> Where did my attention go?\n\nand:\n\n> What relationships in the image may have encouraged it to go there?\n\n---\n\nBefore beginning the puzzle, think of an image you remember clearly. Which part did you notice first, and what made that part different from everything around it?\n",
      "mediaType": "text/markdown"
    }
  },
  "lenses": [
    {
      "@id": "#lens-depends-on-relationship",
      "@type": "Lens",
      "id": "depends-on-relationship",
      "prompt": "Which concepts work mainly through a relationship between different parts of an image?",
      "targets": [
        "contrast",
        "scale",
        "overlap",
        "symmetry",
        "asymmetry",
        "repetition"
      ],
      "explanation": "These concepts are relational. Contrast requires a difference, scale compares sizes, overlap places forms in front of one another, symmetry and asymmetry compare distributions, and repetition requires recurrence.",
      "reasons": {
        "contrast": "Contrast exists when one visual quality differs noticeably from another.",
        "scale": "Scale describes size in relation to another object, the image, or the viewer.",
        "overlap": "Overlap establishes a spatial relationship between two or more forms.",
        "symmetry": "Symmetry depends on correspondence between parts arranged around an axis or center.",
        "asymmetry": "Asymmetry becomes meaningful through unequal but still perceptible relationships among parts.",
        "repetition": "Repetition requires a form, color, interval, or other feature to recur."
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "#lens-can-direct-eye-travel",
      "@type": "Lens",
      "id": "can-direct-eye-travel",
      "prompt": "Which concepts can encourage the viewer's eye to travel from one area of an image to another?",
      "targets": [
        "visual hierarchy",
        "leading lines",
        "diagonal lines",
        "repetition",
        "implied motion",
        "rhythm"
      ],
      "explanation": "Eye movement can be guided by an order of prominence, directional lines, recurring features, and cues that imply movement. The viewer is not mechanically controlled, but the composition makes some visual paths more inviting than others.",
      "reasons": {
        "visual hierarchy": "A hierarchy establishes an order in which some areas attract attention before others.",
        "leading lines": "Leading lines point or curve toward another part of the composition.",
        "diagonal lines": "Diagonals often create a strong directional path across the image.",
        "repetition": "Repeated features encourage the eye to seek and follow their recurrence.",
        "implied motion": "A depicted action or unstable position encourages the eye to follow its apparent direction.",
        "rhythm": "Rhythm organizes repeated visual events into a perceptible sequence."
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "#lens-can-create-emphasis",
      "@type": "Lens",
      "id": "can-create-emphasis",
      "prompt": "Which concepts can make one area appear more important or prominent than another?",
      "targets": [
        "focal point",
        "contrast",
        "isolation",
        "scale",
        "visual hierarchy",
        "visual weight"
      ],
      "explanation": "Prominence can be created directly through a focal point or indirectly through difference, separation, relative size, perceived weight, and an overall order of importance.",
      "reasons": {
        "focal point": "A focal point is an area made especially prominent within a composition.",
        "contrast": "A strong difference can make one area stand out from its surroundings.",
        "isolation": "Separating a form from nearby activity can make it conspicuous.",
        "scale": "An unusually large or small form may attract attention through relative size.",
        "visual hierarchy": "Visual hierarchy organizes elements according to perceived importance.",
        "visual weight": "An area with greater visual weight attracts and holds more attention."
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "#lens-can-suggest-instability",
      "@type": "Lens",
      "id": "can-suggest-instability",
      "prompt": "Which concepts can make a composition feel active, unstable, or in motion?",
      "targets": [
        "asymmetry",
        "visual weight",
        "diagonal lines",
        "implied motion",
        "rhythm"
      ],
      "explanation": "Unequal weight, diagonal direction, depicted action, and rhythmic recurrence can make a composition feel less settled. None always produces instability; the effect depends on how it is used with the rest of the image.",
      "reasons": {
        "asymmetry": "Unequal distribution can create tension when the composition does not feel completely settled.",
        "visual weight": "A strong concentration of visual weight can make the surrounding arrangement feel pulled or unbalanced.",
        "diagonal lines": "Diagonals often appear less static than horizontal or vertical lines.",
        "implied motion": "Implied motion presents a form as acting, falling, turning, or continuing beyond a frozen instant.",
        "rhythm": "Rhythm can create visual momentum through recurring forms and intervals."
      }
    }
  ],
